Helios vkd3d-proton fork: a failed d3d12_device_init releases the instance

d3d12_device_init() takes a reference on the vkd3d instance first, but the
error label that drops it sat above the lock and mutex labels, so every
failure from vkd3d_create_vk_device() on (no physical device, vkCreateDevice
failing) jumped past the decref, and the mutex-init failure destroyed locks
that were never initialized. The leaked reference kept the process-wide
instance singleton alive: the next vkd3d_create_device() in the process was
handed the old VkInstance and ignored its own instance create info.

In the Helios D3D12 UMD that made the Venus fallback after a failed NVK
device run on the NVK instance again ("No physical device available" twice,
D3D12CreateDevice 0x887a0004). The instance reference is now released last,
after the locks and the mutex, on every failure path.

diff --git a/libs/vkd3d/device.c b/libs/vkd3d/device.c
--- a/libs/vkd3d/device.c
+++ b/libs/vkd3d/device.c
@@ -11918,14 +11918,14 @@
     d3d12_device_destroy_vkd3d_queues(device);
     vk_procs = &device->vk_procs;
     VK_CALL(vkDestroyDevice(device->vk_device, NULL));
-out_free_instance:
-    vkd3d_instance_decref(device->vkd3d_instance);
 out_free_fragment_output_lock:
     rwlock_destroy(&device->fragment_output_lock);
 out_free_vertex_input_lock:
     rwlock_destroy(&device->vertex_input_lock);
 out_free_mutex:
     pthread_mutex_destroy(&device->mutex);
+out_free_instance:
+    vkd3d_instance_decref(device->vkd3d_instance);
     return hr;
 }

